# 2. JEENO — Air-Launched Space Launcher

**JEENO — Jet Enabled Efficient Navigational Orbiter**

**Institution:** Politecnico di Milano  
**Course:** Launch Systems  
**Academic Year:** 2024–2025  
**Team:** 9 members  
**My Primary Responsibility:** Aerodynamic Subsystem

---

## Project Overview

JEENO was a conceptual study of a **reusable air-launched orbital launch system** designed to inject small payloads into Low Earth Orbit.

The final architecture consisted of a **two-stage-to-orbit launcher** carried by a civil aircraft and released in flight before rocket-powered ascent.

The project covered the complete preliminary system-design process, including:

- mission and system requirements;
- carrier-aircraft selection;
- launcher sizing;
- structures;
- propulsion;
- aerodynamics;
- trajectory simulation;
- navigation and control;
- orbit insertion;
- uncertainty analysis;
- stage recovery;
- re-entry assessment;
- cost and feasibility analysis.

---

## Mission Concept

The baseline mission targeted insertion of approximately **250 kg payload into a 400 km Sun-Synchronous Orbit**.

The launch architecture used a civil carrier aircraft to provide the initial launch altitude and velocity before vehicle release.

The selected carrier platform was the **Boeing 747-200SF**, following a trade study considering performance, certification risk, acquisition cost and operational flexibility.

---

## My Contribution — Aerodynamic Subsystem

My primary technical responsibility was the **aerodynamic subsystem**.

The aerodynamic work included:

- aerodynamic model development;
- lift and drag estimation;
- centre-of-pressure calculation;
- launcher stability assessment;
- fin sizing;
- aerodynamic configuration trade-offs;
- high-Mach modelling;
- validation against dedicated aerospace tools.

The aerodynamic model was designed to support both the launcher sizing process and the trajectory simulation.

---

## Aerodynamic Modelling

The aerodynamic model calculated:

- lift coefficient;
- drag coefficient;
- centre of pressure;
- stability characteristics;
- aerodynamic loads over the flight envelope.

Different methods were used depending on the Mach-number regime.

### Low-to-Moderate Supersonic Regime

Semi-empirical and analytical methods were used to estimate aerodynamic characteristics.

These included:

- slender-body methods;
- component drag build-up;
- skin-friction estimation;
- base-drag models;
- body–tail interference approximations.

### High-Mach Regime

A dedicated **panel-based aerodynamic algorithm** was developed for high-Mach conditions.

The model used:

- geometry discretisation;
- Newtonian theory;
- modified Newtonian theory;
- local panel-normal calculations;
- pressure integration;
- force transformation into aerodynamic axes.

The method was used to estimate aerodynamic coefficients for the axisymmetric launcher body.

---

## Validation

The aerodynamic model was cross-checked against:

- **RASAero**
- **OpenRocket**

This provided independent verification of the analytical and numerical methods before integration into the mission simulator.

---

## Stability and Control

The launcher had to remain aerodynamically stable during the initial free-flight phase after release from the carrier.

The design therefore included **four tail fins**, selected through iterative aerodynamic and mission-analysis studies.

The fins provided:

- passive stability after release;
- aerodynamic control authority;
- support for the initial pitch-up manoeuvre;
- compatibility with the available carrier-aircraft installation envelope.

Movable fin surfaces were included to assist trajectory control before thrust-vector control became dominant.

---

## Multidisciplinary Integration

The aerodynamic subsystem interacted directly with:

- **Mission Analysis** — aerodynamic forces and moments for trajectory propagation;
- **Structures** — aerodynamic loads for structural sizing;
- **Propulsion** — trajectory and thrust requirements;
- **Recovery** — re-entry aerodynamic and deceleration conditions;
- **System Design** — launcher geometry and carrier integration.

The project therefore required continuous iteration between subsystem models rather than sequential independent design.

---

## Mission Simulation

The complete launcher trajectory was propagated using a **3-DOF simulation environment** incorporating:

- vehicle position and velocity;
- attitude;
- aerodynamic forces;
- thrust;
- mass variation;
- atmospheric models;
- stage separation;
- propulsion events;
- control logic;
- orbit insertion.

A Monte Carlo analysis was also performed to investigate orbit-insertion sensitivity to uncertainties in parameters including:

- thrust;
- propellant mass;
- aerodynamic coefficients;
- recovery-system deployment conditions.

---

## Final System

The final JEENO configuration consisted of:

- **two stages**;
- air-launch from a Boeing 747-200SF;
- liquid propulsion;
- first-stage recovery;
- aerodynamic stabilisation and control fins;
- dedicated orbit-insertion capability.

The nominal design demonstrated the capability to insert a **250 kg payload into a 400 km orbit** under the assumptions of the preliminary model.

---

